  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B007",
        "laureates": [
          "John J. Croat",
          "Masato Sagawa"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention of neodymium-iron-boron permanent magnets"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B009",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have not changed my configuration. B007 identifies a distinct, experimentally realized magnetic-material invention whose exceptional properties have been reproduced, physically characterized, and put to durable use over decades. Its importance is not merely that later products became successful: the original Nd-Fe-B advance created a new permanent-magnet regime with consequences for motors, electronics, and energy technology. Croat and Sagawa remain a potentially proper compact attribution because each is proposed for an original, decisive development rather than for subsequent optimization. This is a mature invention with manifest benefit, provided that the historical record confirms those exact roles.",
    "case_against": "The serious objection remains one of both category and credit. We must establish that the original work was a physics-relevant materials invention rather than principally industrial engineering, and that the Croat and Sagawa routes were genuinely independent and indispensable. The supplied discussion does not resolve those archival questions. Nor should a citation absorb later alloy, processing, manufacturing, or application advances that belong to a much broader community.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson places B007 first, stressing its mature magnetic-material breakthrough and compact attribution, while requiring scrutiny of its status as physics and of the chronology of the parallel developments.",
        "response": "I agree with both reservations and regard them as conditions for proceeding, not minor qualifications. The material's lasting deployment supports maturity, but cannot substitute for evidence that Croat and Sagawa made the original scientific inventions. If that evidence fails, the apparent elegance of the two-name configuration would be misleading."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors B009 because Katori's magic-wavelength principle and Ye's experimental development form a clearly delimited, independently reproduced precision-measurement achievement.",
        "response": "This is the strongest alternative and has reinforced my respect for B009. Its reproducible concept-to-instrument pathway is exemplary. I nevertheless retain B007 narrowly because the original material invention has already transformed the attainable magnetic-energy regime and its practical consequences are fully established. B009 should remain immediately available if the historical independence or Physics-Prize framing of B007 cannot be sustained."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction is foundational, but acknowledges that useful-scale fully fault-tolerant quantum computation has not yet been demonstrated and that its credit is necessarily selective.",
        "response": "The conceptual achievement is major, but this distinction between mature principles and an unrealized defining technological promise matters when comparing candidates. B007 has a repeatedly observed and long-deployed material result. That does not settle the category question for magnets, but it makes its experimental maturity unusually secure."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "What primary historical evidence establishes the priority, independence, and indispensable original roles of John J. Croat and Masato Sagawa?",
      "Can the original Nd-Fe-B achievement be stated as a fundamental materials invention in physics without crediting later industrial optimization?",
      "Whether archival review of B009 would show that Hidetoshi Katori and Jun Ye alone exhaust the indispensable origins of the optical-lattice-clock architecture."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
